# intent-longbox environment — copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit real values.
# Real secrets live in SOPS-encrypted files per the estate standard; this file
# documents the variable NAMES only.

# --- Core ---
# THE VALUES BELOW ARE THE LOCAL DEV STACK, NOT PLACEHOLDERS (E02-B10). They point
# at `docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yml up -d`: port 54329 (not 5432, which
# nothing in this repo ever listens on), database `longbox` (created by
# docker/postgres-init/01-dev-database.sql), and the throwaway role passwords from
# docker/postgres-init/00-roles.sql. Those passwords ARE NOT SECRETS and must never
# become one — the container is bound to 127.0.0.1 for the test lane. Production
# roles are provisioned by the deploy contract with passwords held in SOPS.
# Until this bead the file documented port 5432 and a database nothing created, so
# `pnpm migrate` against a fresh checkout failed twice over.
#
# TWO Postgres URLs, deliberately different roles (E02-D06, 041 §9.2 item 2).
#
# DATABASE_URL is the APPLICATION role. It owns no table and is not a superuser,
# so it cannot `SET session_replication_role` and cannot
# `ALTER TABLE … DISABLE TRIGGER` — which is what makes the append-only guarantee
# (locked decision 4) something the database enforces rather than something the
# server promises. The server asserts this at boot and refuses to serve if the
# role it connects as owns any append-only table.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://longbox_app:longbox_app@127.0.0.1:54329/longbox
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL is the SCHEMA-OWNING role. `pnpm migrate`,
# `pnpm grant-app-role` and `pnpm register-shop` use it; nothing serving HTTP does.
# Unset in a non-production shell it falls back to DATABASE_URL with a loud
# warning; with NODE_ENV=production the fallback is refused.
MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://longbox_migrate:longbox_migrate@127.0.0.1:54329/longbox
# Optional: the app role's NAME, when a deployment does not call it longbox_app.
# Read by the grant step only; passwords never appear here (values live in SOPS).
# APP_DB_ROLE=longbox_app
# HTTP listen port
PORT=3000
# Directory for uploaded scan photos (v0: local disk).
# NOT SERVED STATICALLY. E03-D05 deleted the public mount (046 §3.3 B5); photos
# leave this box only through the tenant-scoped route
# GET /api/v1/shops/:shopId/scan-sessions/:id/photos/:photoId.
UPLOADS_DIR=uploads

# --- Upload guard (E03-B07) ---
# EVERY VALUE HERE IS A PROVISIONAL SAFETY FLOOR, NOT A MEASUREMENT (042 §8.4's
# class, 021 B16): they are set so ordinary counter work cannot reach them and a
# loop or a bomb reaches them immediately. Never quoted as a supported
# resolution, capacity or volume. Defaults live in src/services/media.ts and are
# what the server uses when these are unset; setting one only TIGHTENS or widens
# a floor deliberately, in an environment where that is written down.
#
# THE SERVER REFUSES TO BOOT if any of these is raised above 4x its default
# (src/config.ts, `assertMediaPolicyOrThrow`). A ceiling that far up does not
# tune the control, it disables it — MEDIA_MAX_PIXELS=99999999999 used to switch
# the decompression-bomb guard off in silence. Lowering any of them is always
# allowed; raising a DEFAULT is a code change with its derivation written down.
#
# The decompression-bomb ceiling. 80 MP is ~1.6x the largest sensor in the pilot
# cohort (035 §9's 50 MP Galaxy A56); the per-side ceiling exists because area
# alone accepts a 1 x 400,000,000 strip.
MEDIA_MAX_PIXELS=80000000
MEDIA_MAX_DIMENSION=20000
# Per-session and per-shop storage ceilings. A session photographs ONE book
# (004), so two dozen frames is several times any real need; the shop ceilings
# are what stop an unattended loop from filling the volume 024 §4 puts uploads on.
MEDIA_SESSION_PHOTO_LIMIT=24
MEDIA_SESSION_BYTE_LIMIT=157286400
MEDIA_SHOP_PHOTO_LIMIT=50000
MEDIA_SHOP_BYTE_LIMIT=53687091200

# --- LLM providers (BYOK) ---
# Default provider key (Anthropic / Claude is the default and reference provider)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Pinned default vision model
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# OpenAI-compatible provider (optional alternative adapter)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

# Generic gateway override (jrig Transport lore) — when set, WINS over the
# per-provider settings above and routes the active adapter through it.
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=

# Confidence band thresholds (0..1). high >= BAND_HIGH, medium >= BAND_MEDIUM, else low.
BAND_HIGH=0.85
BAND_MEDIUM=0.5

# --- Pricing (dual sources: PriceCharting historical FMV + eBay live asks) ---
# PriceCharting API token (Premium). Empty = stub client (no live comps).
PRICECHARTING_TOKEN=
# eBay Browse API app credentials (OAuth2 client-credentials; the app token is
# fetched and cached automatically). BOTH empty = stub client (no live asks).
EBAY_CLIENT_ID=
EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional: sandbox host (default https://api.ebay.com) and category filter
# (default 259104 = Comics & Graphic Novels).
# EBAY_BASE_URL=https://api.sandbox.ebay.com

# --- Shopify (per-store Dev Dashboard app for the pilot) ---
# Empty = stub client (no live drafts). Admin API access token, store domain,
# pinned API version.
SHOPIFY_STORE_DOMAIN=
SHOPIFY_ADMIN_TOKEN=
SHOPIFY_API_VERSION=2025-07

# --- Outbox drain (E02-D07, 043 §5.3, §7.1) ---
# FOUR PROVISIONAL FLOORS AND ONE INTERVAL. These are NOT measurements, are
# never quoted as reliability, throughput or latency in any artifact at any
# class (021 B16), and support no claim of fact. They are circuit-breaker
# floors adopted under 042 A3 ("018 A3 caps claims of fact, not safety
# floors"). They close by measurement across the 019 §5 pilot batches,
# SEGMENTED by whether a real Shopify credential was configured — a stub client
# never fails, so a mixed sample would report a reliability belonging to the
# stub. 018 C3's red line: a ceiling may be RAISED freely because it binds
# less; LOWERING one after seeing a result it would change needs a 006 row.
# Unset everywhere = the defaults in src/services/outbox.ts.
# OUTBOX_MAX_ATTEMPTS=6
# OUTBOX_BACKOFF_BASE_MS=30000
# OUTBOX_BACKOFF_CEILING_MS=1800000
# ATTEMPT VISIBILITY: the in-flight window. ⚠ It must exceed the worst-case
# CLOCK SKEW across every host that runs a worker (043 A7) — every timestamp in
# the design is server-side now(), and beyond a single host NTP or a monotonic
# source is a requirement, not an operational nicety.
# OUTBOX_ATTEMPT_VISIBILITY_MS=300000
# OUTBOX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000
# OUTBOX_BATCH_SIZE=10

# --- Shopify dev-store upsert-consistency check (A11, OPEN) ---
# 043 A11 signs ONE assumption OPEN: that a `customId` written by a productSet
# CREATE is immediately findable by a productSet upsert issued moments later. A
# metafield-backed lookup that were eventually consistent would make a fast
# retry create a SECOND product — the exact failure the key exists to prevent —
# and no citation can settle it, because the question is about behaviour and
# not documentation. It closes by MEASUREMENT against an ISOLATED DEV STORE,
# before the saga serves a real shop.
#
# Set all three and run `pnpm test:integration` to execute
# tests/integration/shopify-customid-consistency.test.ts; it SKIPS otherwise.
# ⚠ NEVER point these at a live shop: the test creates a real product.
# SHOPIFY_DEV_STORE_DOMAIN=
# SHOPIFY_DEV_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# SHOPIFY_DEV_API_VERSION=2025-07

# --- Per-shop credential overrides (BYOK seam) ---
# shop_credentials.key_ref stores the NAME of an env var, never a raw key.
#
# E03-D01 (046 §5 A7/A15) MADE THE NAMING A RULE RATHER THAN A CONVENTION. Every
# key_ref must be one of SIX names per shop — `LONGBOX_<SLUG>_` followed by
# ANTHROPIC_KEY, OPENAI_KEY, SHOPIFY_KEY, PRICECHARTING_KEY, EBAY_KEY or
# EBAY_KEY_SECRET — enforced by the CHECK in migrations/014, by the Zod contract,
# and by `resolveKeyRef(keyRef, shopSlug)`, which tests EXACT MEMBERSHIP before it
# reads the environment. The older `SHOP_<SLUG>_<KIND>` names are gone; re-run
# `pnpm register-shop` for any shop still carrying one.
#
# ⚠ THE SLUG'S SEPARATORS ARE STRIPPED, NOT UNDERSCORED: shop `gotham-city` owns
# `LONGBOX_GOTHAMCITY_…`, never `LONGBOX_GOTHAM_CITY_…`. That second spelling was
# a real defect — it let shop `gotham` read shop `gotham-city`'s variable through
# a prefix test — and it is now refused at every layer.
#
# THERE IS NO GLOBAL FALLBACK FOR A SHOP THAT HAS CREDENTIAL ROWS. Leaving one
# of these unset makes the vision provider REFUSE for that shop and the
# Shopify/PriceCharting/eBay clients STUB. The global vars above are the default
# for a shop that declares NO row at all — never a silent substitute for a shop's
# own key.
# LONGBOX_GOTHAM_ANTHROPIC_KEY=
# LONGBOX_GOTHAM_SHOPIFY_KEY=
# LONGBOX_GOTHAM_PRICECHARTING_KEY=
# eBay is a credential PAIR: the key_ref names the client-ID var; the secret
# lives at `${key_ref}_SECRET` (still names-in-DB, raw keys only in env/SOPS).
# LONGBOX_GOTHAM_EBAY_KEY=
# LONGBOX_GOTHAM_EBAY_KEY_SECRET=

# --- Gateway override scope (E03-D01, 046 §11 I5) ---
# LLM_BASE_URL + LLM_API_KEY outrank EVERY per-shop credential, so the pair is
# validated at boot: set both or neither, and the host must be a registered
# provider host (api.anthropic.com, api.openai.com, api.ebay.com,
# api.sandbox.ebay.com). A self-hosted gateway is added here explicitly —
# comma-separated hosts — rather than by disabling the check.
# LONGBOX_GATEWAY_HOST_ALLOWLIST=gateway.internal.example

# --- Identity and sessions (E03-D09; 048 §9.2 R6, §5.1 R9) ---
#
# LONGBOX_PIN_PEPPER — REQUIRED. The server refuses to boot without it.
#
# Every operator PIN is stored as argon2id over (pin ‖ pepper), and the pepper is
# read from the process environment and NEVER written to the database, to a
# migration, to a fixture or to a backup of this database. For a six-digit PIN it
# is close to load-bearing: a million-entry keyspace is enumerable offline
# against argon2id in time that is merely expensive, and 048 §3.5's whole
# "possession-bound channel" argument evaporates the moment the verification can
# be done offline — because a pg_dump is not a phone behind a counter.
#
# ⚠ LOSING IT INVALIDATES EVERY PIN IN THE SYSTEM. That makes it a backup and
# custody obligation (048 §12.4 row 3, E03-B05), not a value to regenerate when
# a deploy complains. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 48`; store in SOPS.
# LONGBOX_PIN_PEPPER=
#
# LONGBOX_PUBLIC_ORIGIN — the origin(s) this deployment answers on, comma
# separated. It is the FALLBACK half of 048 §5.1's CSRF check: the primary
# mechanism is `Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin`, and this list is consulted only
# when a request carries no `Sec-Fetch-Site` at all (an older browser, a
# non-browser client). Leaving it empty makes that fallback CLOSED, not open.
# LONGBOX_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://longbox.example
#
# --- Local bootstrap ONLY (development; refused when NODE_ENV=production) ---
#
# 034 §4.5 is explicit that onboarding creates no device: a phone is enrolled by
# the people who own it, and seeding a fake "counter phone" would put rows in a
# shop's data nobody at the shop made. Setting LONGBOX_BOOTSTRAP_PIN opts a LOCAL
# `pnpm register-shop` run into enrolling one phone and giving the owner that PIN,
# so a fresh database can run the scan flow at all — without it every shop-scoped
# route is behind a device session nothing can issue, and the first thing anybody
# would do is comment out the authentication hook.
#
# The PIN must satisfy the shop's own rule (six digits, no run, no repeat), and
# the device secret is printed ONCE by the script: the database stores only its
# sha256, so losing it means enrolling again. The real flow — a one-time,
# short-lived, shop-scoped enrollment code redeemed ON the device — is E03-D07's.
# LONGBOX_BOOTSTRAP_PIN=
# LONGBOX_BOOTSTRAP_DEVICE_LABEL=local bootstrap phone
